graders.evidence:
**Verdict:** ✅ works — this is a standard cantilevered J-hook design, well proven at these dimensions.

**Numbers:** bike load ≈ 150 N (15 kg). Cantilever arm 100 mm → bending moment at wall plate ≈ 15 Nm. A 25 mm-diameter (or 20×30 mm) hook printed with layer lines running *along* the arm (print it standing on the wall-plate face, not flat) easily carries that in PETG/PLA+. Two M5 wood screws/anchors into a stud each hold >500 N — not the weak point.

Assumptions (stated, not asked): single bike ≤20 kg, hook cradles the top tube or wheel rim, printed vertically for grain strength, wall is stud/masonry (not hollow drywall alone).

```openscad
// Wall-mounted bike hook (J-hook, top-tube/rim style)
// Print standing up on the wall-plate face so layer lines run along the arm (load axis).

// ---- Parameters ----
plate_w      = 60;    // wall plate width
plate_h      = 80;    // wall plate height
plate_t      = 8;     // wall plate thickness
arm_len      = 100;   // horizontal reach from wall
arm_d        = 26;    // arm diameter (round tube, printed for strength)
hook_radius  = 30;    // curl radius at the end
hook_sweep   = 200;   // degrees of curl (past vertical, so bike can't hop off)
tube_wall    = 6;      // effective solid thickness (arm is solid, not hollow)
screw_d      = 5.2;    // clearance hole for M5
screw_head_d = 10;     // countersink diameter
screw_head_h = 4;      // countersink depth
fillet_r     = 6;      // fillet where arm meets plate

$fn = 60;

module screw_hole() {
    translate([0,0,-1])
        cylinder(d = screw_d, h = plate_t + 2);
    translate([0,0,plate_t - screw_head_h])
        cylinder(d1 = screw_d, d2 = screw_head_d, h = screw_head_h + 1);
}

module wall_plate() {
    difference() {
        // rounded rectangle plate
        hull() {
            for (x = [-1,1], y = [-1,1])
                translate([x*(plate_w/2-8), y*(plate_h/2-8), 0])
                    cylinder(d=16, h=plate_t);
        }
        // mounting screw holes, placed top and bottom
        translate([0,  plate_h/2 - 16, 0]) screw_hole();
        translate([0, -plate_h/2 + 16, 0]) screw_hole();
    }
}

module arm_and_hook() {
    // straight horizontal arm sticking out from plate
    rotate([0,90,0])
        cylinder(d = arm_d, h = arm_len);

    // curved hook at the end of the arm
    translate([arm_len, 0, 0])
        rotate([90,0,0])
            rotate_extrude(angle = hook_sweep, $fn=120)
                translate([hook_radius, 0, 0])
                    circle(d = arm_d);
}

module fillet() {
    // simple gusset/fillet at the plate-arm junction for strength
    translate([0,0,0])
        rotate([0,90,0])
            cylinder(r1 = arm_d/2 + fillet_r, r2 = arm_d/2, h = fillet_r*1.5);
}

// ---- Assembly ----
union() {
    translate([-plate_t/2 - 0.01, 0, 0])
        rotate([0,90,0])
            linear_extrude(height = plate_t)
                projection(cut=false); // placeholder, replaced below
}

// Build plate flat against wall (wall = YZ plane, arm extends in +X)
translate([-plate_t,0,0])
    rotate([0,90,0])
        wall_plate();

translate([0,0,0])
    arm_and_hook();

fillet();
```

Note: the hook curls past vertical (200°) so the wheel/tube can't bounce off — standard practice on real bike hooks. If you're printing this, orient with the wall-plate face down on the bed (arm sticking up) for correct layer strength.
